played for 21 hours, won two normal level rounds, the gameplay were filled with excitements.
This game is very similar to civilization v/vi, but with a lot of interesting new elements:
a senate and government that can change due to player choices and development, and can affect many aspects of your civilization;
a unique battleship module research/unlock/modification feature;
quests and random encounters make the game nonlinear, leaving decisive choices for the player;
a hero assignment feature. each hero has his/her unique upgrade tree, and they can excel at either leading fleets/managing system/leading senate. this is way more fun than civ v/vi’s all-the-same generals/scientists which can only be consumed to do the same thing;
exquisitely made UI that looks beautiful all way round.
Like civ v/vi, it takes very long time to finish one whole game (usually 10+ hours), requiring a lot of patience and study on the strategy and the tech tree. and learning the tech tree is a little frustrating, as the names of these techs sound too si-fi, as in contrast to civ’s tech tree. use the search bar as much as possible.
as the game progresses, especially when you play with 6+ AIs, it tends to slow down significantly when you hit next turn as your rig is computing the moves of all other players. something to improve.
from my perspective, its one of the best 4x games on the market.

